---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import DeploymentStrategiesReference from '../../components/DeploymentStrategiesReference.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Blue-Green vs Canary Deployment — Which to Use" description="Compare blue-green, canary, rolling, and feature-flag deployment strategies and their trade-offs.">
  <DeploymentStrategiesReference client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Blue-Green vs Canary Deployment: Key Differences</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th><th>Blue-Green</th><th>Canary</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Traffic shift</td><td>All-at-once switch</td><td>Gradual, percentage-based</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Rollback speed</td><td>Instant (switch back)</td><td>Fast, but some users already affected</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Infrastructure cost</td><td>Double capacity during switch</td><td>Minimal extra capacity</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Monitoring requirement</td><td>Lower</td><td>Higher — needs real-time metrics per version</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Blast radius if broken</td><td>100% of users, briefly</td><td>Small % of users, contained</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Blue-green favors simplicity and instant rollback at the cost of running duplicate infrastructure; canary favors gradual risk exposure but needs stronger observability to be effective.</p>

  <h2>Why deployment strategy is a real design decision</h2>
  <p>How you roll out a new version affects your blast radius when something goes wrong, your infrastructure cost during deployment, and how quickly you can roll back. There's no universally "best" strategy — each trades off differently between safety, speed, and complexity.</p>

  <h2>How to use this reference</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Weigh your priorities: instant rollback vs. infrastructure cost vs. gradual risk exposure.</li>
    <li>Match your team's monitoring maturity to the strategy's requirements (canary needs good real-time metrics to be effective).</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common Deployment Strategy Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Choosing canary deployment without adequate monitoring</strong> — canary's whole value depends on detecting problems in the small subset of traffic before expanding rollout.</li>
    <li><strong>Using blue-green without database migration compatibility planning</strong> — schema changes need to work with both old and new code versions during the transition window.</li>
    <li><strong>Never cleaning up stale feature flags</strong>, accumulating technical debt that makes the codebase harder to reason about over time.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Reference Deployment Strategies</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Choosing a deployment strategy for a new critical service</li>
    <li>Explaining tradeoffs to stakeholders when proposing a deployment pipeline change</li>
    <li>Diagnosing why a recent deployment caused more disruption than expected</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Blue-Green vs Canary FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Which is better, blue-green or canary deployment?</strong> Neither is universally better — blue-green offers instant, all-at-once rollback with simpler mechanics; canary offers smaller blast radius but requires stronger real-time monitoring to catch issues before wider rollout.</p>
  <p><strong>Do I need both extra infrastructure and canary tooling?</strong> Not necessarily — blue-green needs double capacity briefly during the switch, while canary needs traffic-splitting and monitoring tooling rather than duplicated infrastructure.</p>
  <p><strong>Can these strategies be combined?</strong> Yes — many teams combine feature flags with canary or rolling deployments, decoupling code deployment from feature activation for even finer control.</p>
</ToolLayout>
